[model] lru_ttl_cache: A comprehensive, thread-safe LRU cache with Time-To-Live (TTL) expiry.
[model] 
[model] This module provides a robust implementation of a Least Recently Used (LRU) cache
[model] that supports expiration based on Time-To-Live (TTL) values. It is designed to be
[model] thread-safe, allowing concurrent access from multiple threads without explicit locking
[model] by the user. The implementation uses a combination of a doubly-linked list for
[model] LRU ordering and a dictionary for O(1) lookups, along with a threading.Lock for
[model] mutual exclusion.
[model] 
[model] Key Features:
[model] - LRU eviction policy: When the cache is full, the least recently used item is evicted.
[model] - TTL expiry: Items expire after a specified duration, regardless of access frequency.
[model] - Thread safety: All operations are atomic with respect to each other.
[model] - Configurable capacity: The maximum number of items the cache can hold.
[model] - Customizable TTL units: TTL can be specified in seconds, minutes, hours, etc.
[model] - Statistics tracking: Tracks hits, misses, evictions, and expirations.
[model] - Async support: Includes an async-compatible interface for use with asyncio.
[model] 
[model] Usage Example:
[model]     cache = LRUTLCache(max_size=100, ttl_seconds=3600)
[model]     cache.set('key', 'value', ttl=10)
[model]     value = cache.get('key')  # Returns 'value' if not expired
[model]     cache.delete('key')
[model]     cache.clear()
[model] 
[model] Thread Safety:
[model]     This cache is thread-safe. Multiple threads can call set(), get(), delete(),
[model]     and clear() simultaneously. The internal locking mechanism ensures that
[model]     operations are atomic.
[model] 
[model] TTL Handling:
[model]     TTL is stored as a Unix timestamp (seconds since epoch). When an item is
[model]     retrieved or updated, the TTL is refreshed. When an item is deleted or
[model]     expired, the entry is removed from the cache.
[model] 
